  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nElevated convection is ongoing across western Kansas early this\nmorning. This activity is expected to track southeastward across\nthe area, bringing primarily a heavy rain risk. However, MUCAPE\n1000-1500 J/kg per mesoanalysis suggests storms could yield a \nfew instances of small hail and gusty winds. The overall hail\nthreat is being kept at bay due to moisure-laden nature of the \nvertical profile with PWATs around the 90th percentile per 00z \nDDC skew-T. Areas to the north of the convection, mainly along\nRoute 96 northward along the I-70 corridor, are expected to see\nfog develop this morning as well. Visibilities are expected to\ndrop as low as 1-3 miles. However, the REFS ensemble does\nsuggest probabilities less than a half-mile in the 40-60% range,\nso the signal for denser fog is evident, but ultimately held off\non any dense fog headlines after collaboration with neighboring\noffices. Trends will be monitored throughout the morning for the\nneed for headlines.\n\nAnother small chance of storms is evident across south-central\nKansas this evening and overnight. Coverage of any storms is\nexpected to be limited, with POPs largely 25% or less this\nevening and into Friday morning. Any severe weather risk is\nexpected to be limited given loosely organized convection.\nHowever, a stronger storm, especially in the evening, could\nproduce stronger winds and perhaps some hail.\n\nTemperatures are expected to again increase beginning today and\ninto this weekend across the forecast area. Temperatures return\nto the 90s this afternoon. With dew points in the low 70s across\nsouth-central Kansas, heat indices could get in the 100-104\nrange, just below heat advisory criteria. However, increasing\ntemperatures back around 100-105 and and elevated dew points \nalong the KS/OK border could yield heat indices to at least \nreach heat advisory criteria for Barber County Friday and beyond.\nHowever, any headlines will occur in later forecast updates and\nwill likely be a day by day decision.",
